  "narrative": "The metabolomics data reveal a strong perturbation of **galactose metabolic and galactosemia pathways** (KEGG map00052 / SMP00043 and SMP00182; FDR = 0.0, fold-enrichment ≈ 951). Six input metabolites—D-galactose (C00221), galactitol (C01697), galactinol (C01235), sucrose (C00089), myo-inositol (C00137), and glycerol (C00116)—anchor this cluster.\n\nThe most mechanistically informative driver is **galactitol**, the sugar alcohol that accumulates in classic galactosemia when galactose cannot be further metabolized via the Leloir pathway. Pathway-membership queries confirm galactitol is annotated in both the Galactose Metabolism (map00052) and Galactosemia (SMP00182) entries. **Galactinol**, the galactosyl-donor upstream of galactitol, and **myo-inositol**, a downstream product of inositol metabolism that intersects galactose chemistry, are likewise confirmed members of the same pathway entries.\n\nA KEGG graph traversal confirms a reachable (bidirectional) path from **D-glucose (C00221)** to **galactitol (C01697)** in five steps via glyceraldehyde (C00267) and other Leloir intermediates, suggesting glucose serves as the upstream precursor driving galactitol accumulation under conditions of pathway bottleneck.\n\nA secondary but significant signal appears in the **glycerophospholipid biosynthetic pathway** (Wikipathways WP2533; FDR = 7.9 × 10⁻⁵). Here, the driver compounds are **ethanolamine (C00189)**, **myo-inositol (C00137)**, and **glycerol (C00116)**, all confirmed members. Ethanolamine is annotated in phosphatidylcholine biosynthesis (SMP14212) and links glycerol to phospholipid head-group generation; KEGG graph analysis, however, found no direct path from ethanolamine to myo-inositol within six hops, suggesting these metabolites may co-regulate glycerophospholipid metabolism through parallel, rather than sequential, reactions.\n\n**Biological significance.** The convergence on galactose metabolism points to impaired galactose catabolism or a surge in galactose flux in the treatment state, consistent with inborn metabolic stress, toxin exposure, or disrupted肠道 microbiota–derived galactose processing. Elevated galactitol is a known osmolite and can promote lens cataractogenesis and neuronal stress if chronic. The concurrent perturbation of glycerophospholipid metabolism implicates membrane-remodeling stress and possibly choline/ethanolamine cycle dysregulation, which affects cell-membrane integrity, signaling lipid production, and neurotransmission—relevant to the observed dopamine (C03758) alteration as well. This multi-pathway pattern suggests a systemic metabolic reconfiguration rather than a single-enzyme defect.",
